> Checking PPP link

To check if the PPP link is active, you can look at the ppp daemon's corresponding pid. You may use the following C code for this purpose:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <string.h>

int is_ppp_active() {
    int res;
    res = system("[ -d /proc/sys/net/ipv4/conf/ppp0 ]");
    return WEXITSTATUS(res) == 0 ? 1 : 0;
}

int main() {
    printf("PPP link is %s\n", is_ppp_active() ? "active" : "inactive");
    return 0;
}
```

This program does a check if the `/proc/sys/net/ipv4/conf/ppp0` directory exists (which is created when the ppp interface is up). If it does, the is_ppp_active() function will return `1`, otherwise `0`.

> Restarting PPP

You can do this by sending proper signals to pppd process (SIGHUP to restart, SIGTERM to stop). Activation can be done by `fork()` and exec functions. Here is the simple implementation using `fork()`, `execl()` and `kill()`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>

void start_ppp() {
    pid_t pid = fork();
    if (pid == 0) {
        execl("/usr/sbin/pppd", "pppd", NULL); // Make sure the path to pppd is correct
    } else if (pid < 0) {
        printf("Error: Unable to fork\n");
    }
}

void stop_ppp() {
    int res;
    res = system("killall -q pppd");
}

void restart_ppp() {
    int res;
    res = system("killall -q -HUP pppd"); // Send SIGHUP to pppd process
}

int main() {
   // You can use the above functions in your code.
   return 0;
}
```

In the above example, `start_ppp()` is starting a new instance of pppd. `stop_ppp()` and `restart_ppp()` are using `system()` function with `killall` command to send signals to pppd process. The `-q` option prevents `killall` from producing output.

